ucture. Since 48 = 16*3, you can use H_16 ⊗ (a 3x3 \"conference matrix\"? no, 3x3 Hadamard doesn't exist). The Kronecker product of Hadamard matrices gives Had(16)⊗Hadamard(3) — no Had(3). But Paley construction gives Had(48) directly as a ±1 matrix; the issue is only speed (no fast Walsh transform), so you'd use a dense 48×48 matmul per head (or 96×96) — for CPU-only, negligible.\n\nHmm, but note: for the *equivalence-preserving* requirement, the Hadamard must commute with RoPE. A generic Had(48) acting on pair indices works because it acts identically on both elements of a pair (as a block-diagonal over pair-index permutation)? Let me define: q ∈ R^96, pairs (i, i+48), i ∈ [0,48). Write q = [a; b] with a,b ∈ R^48. RoPE: R(θ) = [[C, -S],[S, C]] with C=diag(cos θ_i), S=diag(sin θ_i). A matrix M = I_2 ⊗ K with K ∈ R^{48×48} gives M^T R M = [[C, -S],[S,C]] since K K = I if K orthogonal... wait M^T C M = K^T C K ≠ C in general. Hmm, that's wrong: M^T [[C,-S],[S,C]] M = [[K^T C K, -K^T S K],[K^T S K, K^T C K]] which is NOT [[C,-S],[S,C]]. So it doesn't commute. Right — this is exactly the difficulty: the RoPE commutant consists of matrices [[A,-B],[B,A]] with A,B commuting appropriately... The set of matrices M such that M R(θ) = R(θ) M for all θ: writing M = [[P,Q],[R,T]], we need P C - Q S = C P, P S + Q C = C S... For all θ (diagonal entries arbitrary), this forces P,T diagonal-ish complex and Q = -... The commutant is {[[A,-B],[B,A]] : A,B diagonal}? Let's check with complex representation z_i = a_i + i b_i, RoPE multiplies z_i by e^{iθ_i}. The commutant over real matrices = complex diagonal matrices (multiplication by e^{iφ_i} per coordinate) — dimension 96 real = 48 complex. Wait, over the reals, commuting with all diag(e^{iθ_i}) means the complex matrix must be complex-diagonal: M z = diag(e^{iφ_i}) z. So the only rotations commuting with RoPE are per-pair phase rotations — which are NOT Hadamards. So exact commutation with Hadamard is impossible.\n\nThat's why the QuaRot paper does something else. I now recall: QuaRot's solution to RoPE is \"Theorem 3.3 / §3.3: RoPE does not prevent quantization\" — they show empirically and via analysis that applying Hadamard online on q and k (which changes the score computation) is fine because they apply H to BOTH q and k, and use the argument that H^T R H ≈ ... no.\n\nActually — I'm now fairly confident the real QuaRot design: W_q and W_k are rotated by an offline Hadamard *within each head* (they call it \"offline Hadamard applied to q and k to make their quantization noise isotropic, and they prove that the resulting change in attention output is bounded and does not depend on the specific Hadamard structure\" — because the rotation applied to q and k before RoPE is equivalent to having a different set of RoPE frequencies / different trained weights, and empirically the model is unchanged because RoPE is applied to the rotated vectors anyway; the \"exact equivalence\" isn't claimed for that piece, only \"no loss\" empirically). Hmm, but QuaRot claims exact equivalence of the unquantized rotated model (they verify rotated model ppl = baseline ppl). For exactness on q/k they'd need the rotation to be undone before RoPE... \n\nLet me think about what preserves exactness: if you rotate q → Hq and k → Hk with the same H (per-head), then apply RoPE, then scores = (R H q)·(R' H k) = q^T H^T R^T R' H k. In the baseline it's q^T R^T R' k. These are equal for all q,k iff H^T R(Δ) H = R(Δ) for all Δ, which as shown requires H = complex-diagonal. So it's NOT exact. UNLESS: H commutes with R in the \"split-half\" convention where H acts within pairs only... no.\n\nHmm, so how does QuaRot claim exactness? I remember now: they state that rotating q and k does NOT preserve the attention output exactly, and they address this with \"Theorem 3.2/3.3: we can apply Hadamard to the RoPE-rotated q and k\" — i.e., apply the online Hadamard AFTER RoPE. Rotating after RoPE: q' = H R q, k' = H R' k → score = q^T R^T H^T H R' k = q^T R^T R' k. EXACT! Yes!! That's it. The Hadamards applied to q and k are ONLINE (at runtime, after RoPE), so they cancel exactly in the dot product, and both q,k are quantized in the rotated basis. And because it's applied at runtime after RoPE, there's no commutation constraint with RoPE at all. The only constraint is that H must be applied per-head (block-diagonal over heads) so blocks are head_dim-sized... and here's where the power-of-2 requirement comes: to have a fast Hadamard of size head_dim (64 or 128 for LLaMA). \n\nHmm wait, but H applied to q of head h and k of head h' must be the same H (per head index) so H^T H = I in the product. Fine, block-diag identical per head.\n\nThen what is \"H_admm\"? That's the offline rotation of v and o_proj: v' = H v (online after RoPE? v doesn't get RoPE). Actually H_admm in QuaRot refers to the Hadamard used for the \"down_proj matrix of MLP\" and for the o_proj: \"H_admm\" = the Hadamard that is applied to the attention output before o_proj (online) and to the MLP activation. And the v-rotation: v is offline-rotated per head? Hmm, v is written into the KV cache, and QuaRot quantizes the KV cache; the v cache rotation is offline (fold H into W_v per head and H^T into W_o per head), exact. Then attention output = P v (rotated per-head), and then online H on the concatenated attention output before o_proj... \n\nWhatever the precise details, for the plan the key technical points about head_dim=96 are:\n(a) The per-head online Hadamard on q/k (post-RoPE) and per-head offline v/o_proj rotation require an orthogonal 96×96 matrix. Options: blkdiag(H_64,H_32), blkdiag(H_32×3), dense Had(96) if available (Hadamard order 96 exists? Order 96 = 4×24, Hadamard conjecture verified up to huge orders; order 96 exists since 96=3×32 and there's a known Had(3×2^k)? Hadamard matrices of order 3·2^k exist (Williamson/Paley type) — actually Had(96) is known to exist (order 96 = 4·24; the Hadamard conjecture has been verified for all multiples of 4 up to at least 664, and 96 is definitely known to exist, e.g., Paley: 96 = 95+1, need 95 a prime power — no. But there's the \"Goethals-Seidel\" etc. I know orders 4·t for t ≤ 300ish are all constructed. So Had(96) exists but you'd need to ship a ±1 96×96 matrix file, which we cannot download offline. Alternative: use a random orthogonal matrix from QR of a Gaussian (deterministic seed) → this is the \"random rotation\" ablation that QuaRot itself compares against (paper shows Hadamard ≈ random rotation but Hadamard is faster). For CPU-only design, dense 96×96 rotations are cheap; that's actually a nice feasibility point: CPU-only makes the dense-matrix fallback acceptable.\n(b) MLP intermediate 8192 = 2^13 → power-of-2 Hadamard fine. Hidden 3072 = 2^10 × 3 → NOT a power of two! So the residual-stream rotation G (size hidden=3072) also breaks the power-of-2 fast Hadamard. LLaMA hidden sizes 4096/8192/5120(7B: 4096; 13B 5120 = 2^10*5 → also not power of two! But QuaRot handles that by using a \"randomized block-diagonal Hadamard\": in QuaRot they note that for dimensions not a power of 2, they use a block-diagonal Hadamard with the largest power-of-2 block plus remaining (that's the \"rotation with non-power-of-2\" handling: e.g., for hidden dim 5120 they use block-diag of H_1024/H_4096?). Actually in QuaRot's `quarot_utils.rotate_model`, they use `H = random_hadamard_matrix(dim)` with assertion `is_pow2`, and for non-pow2 dims they use the \"admm\" rotation `get_rotation_matrix`... The LLaMA-3-8B hidden = 4096 (pow2) so their code works; 70B hidden=8192 pow2; Llama-2 7B/13B hidden 4096/5120 — hmm 13B is 5120. They used `--rotation_mode`... In the QuaRot code there's `Htype: \"had\" | \"had(2)\" | \"random\"` and a function `rotate_matmul(W, is_w, dim, R_type, ...)`; for `had(2)` it's \"block diagonal Hadamard where each block is 2\"? Not sure. I'll present this as an implementation risk to verify in code, plus the concrete fallback: bl