{"id": "0b563837-c136-4936-b930-54edc19226cb", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:47:15.461290", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "d31ae951-fc66-4e26-a4fb-7687042c436b", "thought": [], "reasoning_content": "I have enough inventory. Now produce the plan handoff.\n\nLet me write a thorough, well-organized plan. Keep it dense but structured.\n\nKey content:\n\nA. Inventory (observed)\n- /workspace contains ONLY task.json (499 bytes). No git repo.\n- No QuaRot, GPTQ, lm-eval, or any quantization/eval code anywhere on the mounted filesystem (find over / -xdev for *quarot*, *gptq*, *phi3*, *.safetensors, *.gguf, *.whl → empty).\n- Python 3.14.4 system; 105 packages, all distro/system tooling (ansible, apt, dbus, PIL, Jinja...). NO torch, transformers, datasets, accelerate, numpy, scipy, safetensors, tokenizers, lm-eval. `import torch`/`import numpy` → ModuleNotFoundError.\n- No model weights, no HF cache (~/.cache doesn't exist; /home/actor empty), no datasets (task.json lists datasets: []).\n- Network disabled: DNS resolution fails (gaierror); `pip install numpy` fails (no network/pip broken: FileNotFoundError in pip's downloader). No wheelhouse.\n- Compilers present (gcc-15, g++, make, git, curl/wget binaries) but useless without network.\n- Compute: nproc=224, sched affinity 0–223, no cgroup cpu.max visible; 2 TB RAM (2011 GB, ~1.9 TB avail). The \"2 cores effectively usable\" is a brief/environment claim I cannot confirm from inside; treat as scheduling/policy constraint.\n- Disk: /workspace is an NFS mount at 100% with ~6–7 GB free → too small for Phi-3-mini BF16 weights (~7.9 GB) + activations; /tmp is tmpfs with ~1 TB. So scratch must go to /tmp (RAM-backed) and outputs must be tiny (metrics JSON only).\n- Note Python 3.14 is very new: torch/transformers wheels for 3.14 may not exist for the versions QuaRot pins (repo targets py3.10, torch 2.2–2.3), so an install step is a real dependency risk even if network were available.\n\nSo: everything is absent. Conclusion: design-only, and any execution would need a different environment (GPU box + network + pre-fetched model/datasets). I'll state this plainly.\n\nB. Code-path LLaMA-specificity: I cannot inspect QuaRot source (absent), so statements below are from model knowledge of upstream QuaRot (astone3207/QuaRot, \"QuaRot: Outlier-Free 4-Bit Inference in Rotated LLMs\", NeurIPS 2024), flagged as unverified here. Structure of that repo:\n- Entry points: `main.py` (argparse: --model, --model_args, --rotation_mode {full,none,partial,residual,no_hadmsk?,...}, --quant_method {normal,gptq,smoothquant?}, --w_bits/--a_bits/--v_bits/--k_bits, --quant_output, --quant_input, --quant_attn, --quant_dataset, --nsamples, --tasks, --sessions/--num_fusion_samples, --calib_size), `utils/quarot_utils.py` (rotate_model / merge_ln / rotate matrices / FastHadamardTransform, find_layers with hardcoded model-type key dicts), `utils/gptq_utils/*` (parallel_gptq, gptq_layer, quantizer with `--quant_method` symmetric/asymmetric, groupsize), `utils/data_utils.py` (C4 / ptb / open-llama calibration), `utils/hadamard_matrix.py` (sympy/`get_had` + `assert is_pow2(size)` fast Walsh–Hadamard), plus **model copies**: `models/llama.py`, `models/llama_gptq.py`, `models/llama_smoothquant.py`, `models/opt.py`, `models/mpt.py`, `models/llama_baselines` etc. → this is the crux: QuaRot is NOT model-agnostic. Rotation and quantization hooks are hand-inserted into a copied HF LlamaForCausalLM (GPT-FAQ/SparseGPT lineage), and the model-type dispatch is via hardcoded module-name key lists (`llama_attention_keys`, `llama_mlp_keys`, ...) plus RMSNorm classes named `LlamaRMSNorm`, RoPE helper `rotate_half`, `apply_rotary_pos_emb`.\n- Therefore Phi-3 needs a `models/phi3.py` port + `phi3_attention_keys/mlp_keys` in find_layers + rotation plumbing for Phi3Attention/Phi3MLP classes, plus Phi3 RMSNorm merging (fuse_ln_weight_into_proj) — which is model-agnostic in principle but implemented against Llama class names.\n\nConcrete Phi-3 adaptation items (with config values from memory of microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct config.json — flag as must-verify against the actual config):\narchitectures Phi3ForCausalLM; model_type phi3; hidden_size 3072; intermediate_size 8192; num_hidden_layers 32; num_attention_heads 32; num_key_value_heads 32 → **GQA ratio 1, i.e. plain MHA**; head_dim = 3072/32 = **96** (no explicit head_dim field); vocab_size 32064 (padded), eos 32000/<|end|>; tie_word_embeddings (verify — I believe Phi-3-mini ties lm_head↔embed); max_position_embeddings 4096 with rope_scaling type **longrope** (short_factor/long_factor, mscale ~1.2078, original_max_position_embeddings 4096); rms_norm_eps 1e-5; use_mlp_bias False; use_sliding_window False (sliding_window 2047 present in later revisions); attention uses a scaling factor of 1/sqrt(head_dim)? — actually Phi-3 uses `attention_scaling`/`logit_softcapping` fields; need to verify.\n\nConsequences:\n1. head_dim 96 is the critical breakage. QuaRot's online Hadamards are applied per head on the post-RoPE q and k (so H^T H cancels exactly in the dot product — no commutation constraint with RoPE), and per-head offline rotations for v/o_proj. All of those need a 96×96 Hadamard. Sylvester/fast-Walsh–Hadamard only works for powers of two; upstream code asserts that. Fix options, in preference order:\n   (a) Block-diagonal Hadamard blkdiag(H_32,H_32,H_32) (3 | 96), exact orthogonality, preserves fast transform, weaker mixing than a full 96 Hadamard.\n   (b) Dense known-existing Had(96) (96 = 4·24, existence known) shipped as a ±1 file; no fast transform but negligible on CPU; requires sourcing/generating the matrix (Paley-type constructions don't give 96 directly; would need a Williamson/paired-form construction or a trusted mirror).\n   (c) Random orthogonal (QR of seeded Gaussian) of size 96 — this is the ablation control, not the QuaRot method.\n   Note: the same non-power-of-2 problem hits the **residual/hidden dim 3072 = 3·1024** for the offline \"full\" rotation G — LLaMA-2/3-8B hidden sizes (4096) are powers of two, so upstream code is clean there but Phi-3 is not. Block-diag(H_1024)×3 is the natural analogue (this is also what QuaRot-like ports do for 5120). And the MLP intermediate 8192 = 2^13 → clean Hadamard, so the MLP-side \"Theorem 3.2\" online Hadamard is unaffected.\n2. Fused projections: Phi-3 uses a single `qkv_proj` (output 3·3072 in one Linear) and `gate_up_proj` (2·8192). LLaMA-style per-module rotation code that rotates `q_proj/k_proj/v_proj` and `gate_proj/up_proj` separately must either un-fuse (slice into views) or handle fused weight shapes; per-head rotation of q/k/v inside the fused qkv must be applied to head-aligned slices. This affects both rotation folding and GPTQ group quantization (group boundaries along the input dim are unaffected, but the Hessian/`percdamp` structure is per-module, so fusing is fine for GPTQ; for rotation it matters).\n3. RoPE variant: longrope (long_factor/short_factor + mscale) must survive the port; it does not interact with quantization mathematically (rotations stay in the same place: online H applied post-RoPE), but any custom `apply_rotary_pos_emb` in the copied modeling file must be kept identical between FP16 reference and quantized runs, otherwise the comparison is confounded by a modeling diff rather than quantization. Also `trust_remote_code=True` in the original release vs. native `transformers.models.phi3` — pin one path and use it for both reference and quantized arms.\n4. Tied embeddings / lm_head: if lm_head is tied, rotating the residual stream requires care (the offline G folded into embed_tokens must also be handled in lm_head; QuaRot's LLaMA path assumes untied lm_head). Also lm_head quantization is a separate knob; keep lm_head in FP16 for the main grid.\n5. GQA: contrary to the brief's presumption, Phi-3-mini has num_key_value_heads=32 → GQA ratio 1; so the GQA-repeat path is trivially exercised (no real GQA stress). If the brief wants a genuine GQA test, Phi-3-small-128k-instruct (MoE, GQA) or a second non-LLaMA model with GQA would be needed; LLaMA-3-8B (28/8 = 3.5:1 GQA, head_dim 128) is the natural positive control for the GQA code path. Flag as a factual correction to the brief.\n6. Vocab 32064 and padded embedding rows: no effect on rotation (only hidden-dim matters), but affects embedding-quantization settings and memory.\n7. Outlier structure: Phi-3 was distilled with longer-context RoPE scaling; the \"attention sinks\"/massive-activation pattern differs from LLaMA (Phi-3 still has strong first-token sink behavior). The diagnostics (per-channel kurtosis/max-to-median ratio, activation outlier visualization) are cheap and directly test \"does rotation remove outliers here too\".\n\nC. Experiment design outline (2×2×3 + rotation-structure arm)\n- Factor R (rotation): {Identity (no rotation), QuaRot-Hadamard}. Optional 3rd level R2 = Random-orthogonal same placement (structure control) — strongly recommended because for Phi-3 the Hadamard must be approximated (block-diag 96/3072), so R vs R2 tells whether any residual gap is due to rotation *placement/exactness* vs Hadamard *structure*.\n- Factor A (rounding algo): {RTN (per-channel/per-group round-to-nearest, asymmetric symmetric choice fixed), GPTQ (Hessian-based, seqlen 4096 calibration)}. Note: in QuaRot, `--quant_method normal` ≈ RTN; and GPTQ uses the same rotated model + same calibration set.\n- Factor B (weight bits): {4, 6, 8}, weight-only (a_bits=v_bits=k_bits=0), symmetric/asymmetric fixed (QuaRot default: symmetric per-group for weights; RTN per-channel).\n- Grid: 2 (R) × 2 (A) × 3 (B) = 12 quantized cells (+ 3 optional cells for random-rotation control) + 1 FP16 reference. All cells share the identical pipeline (same calibration set, same eval slices, same code path, same seed).\n- Baselines/controls:\n  * FP16 (BF16) Phi-3-mini reference: upper bound and the \"0-bit\" anchor for Δppl/Δacc.\n  * Non-rotated RTN and non-rotated GPTQ (the R=Identity arm) — these are the essential controls that make the 2×3 interpretable. Without them, RTN-vs-GPTQ differences are confounded with the rotation.\n  * Rotation-only, no quantization (R=QuaRot, bits=16) — exactness check: must reproduce FP16 ppl within numerical noise (<1% relative). This is a hard gate before running the grid; if it fails, the port is wrong.\n  * Groupsize sensitivity for GPTQ at 4 bits {128 (default), -1 (per-channel)} to avoid attributing a groupsize artifact to the algorithm; RTN at per-channel (g=-1) and g=128 to match.\n  * Positive control on the model side: LLaMA-3-8B (or Llama-2-7B) at 4-bit QuaRot-RTN/GPTQ to confirm our port reproduces the published QuaRot numbers (i.e., that our pipeline is not broken), then Phi-3 is a genuine generalization test. Optional/secondary if budget permits.\n  * Negative control: shuffled/random calibration data for GPTQ (should degrade), and a \"rotation applied but not merged\" sanity check.\n- Endpoints:\n  * Primary: WikiText-2 test perplexity, 4096-token non-overlapping windows, no stride (QuaRot/GPTQ-FAQ default), reporting Δppl = ppl(cell) − ppl(FP16) and % degradation; also 2048-context slice for comparability with published QuaRot numbers (they report seqlen 2048 for LLaMA).\n  * Primary zero-shot: piqa, arc_easy, arc_challenge, hellaswag, winogrande, openbookqa (lambada standard is awkward with Phi's chat tokenizer → optional), plus mmlu (57-subject, subset mean) and truthfulqa_mc1/2 and gsm8k (strict-match; gsm8k is known to be extremely quantization-sensitive → good stress test). Use HF `lm_eval` with `phi3` chat-template settings; log both raw template and in-context format, and fix `--num_fewshot` per task (piqa 0, arc 0, hellaswag 0, winogrande 0, truthfulqa 0, mmlu 5-shot, gsm8k 8-shot CoT) to keep the grid tractable.\n  * Downstream aggregation: relative accuracy retention = acc(cell)/acc(FP16) per task, plus the mean over the 6 \"core\" tasks (QuaRot-style average) to give one