rotation arm separates \"rotation placement/exactness\" from \"Hadamard-specific structure\". The no-quant rotation arm separates port/modeling bugs from quantization loss. The groupsize arm separates rounding-algorithm effects from group-wise-representation effects.\n- Fairness rules: identical calibration set+size for RTN and GPTQ (RTN must not see fewer tokens), same dtype, same lm_head/embedding exclusions, same asymmetric/symmetric choice, and report all cells including failures.\n\nD. Feasibility/resource statement (CPU-only)\n- Nothing can run here (see inventory). Design only.\n- If executed on a CPU-only host with 2 usable cores and ~2 TB RAM: Phi-3-mini has 3.8B params; BF16 forward ≈ 7.6 GB weights, so RAM is fine (2 TB), but throughput is the binding constraint. Rough CPU prefill estimate for a 3.8B dense model, 2 cores: ~0.5–2 TFLOP effective (AVX-512/AMX, bf16 GEMM ~100–400 GFLOP/s effective) → ~2·P FLOPs/token ≈ 7.6 GFLOP/token → ~20–50 tokens/s → 400k-token WikiText-2 test ≈ 2–6 h per cell just for ppl; GPTQ calibration (128×4096 forward + per-layer Hessian accumulation + Cholesky solves on 3072/8192 dims) ≈ 2–8 h on CPU (Hessian matmul-heavy, feasible: H is at most 8192×8192, Cholesky ~10^11 flops → seconds-minutes; the forward passes dominate). RTN quantization itself is minutes.\n- Zero-shot battery is the killer: piqa+arc_e+arc_c+hellaswag+winogrande+obqa ≈ 72k prompts ≈ several hundred million tokens of prefill → tens of core-hours per cell at 2 cores; ×12 cells → not feasible in a CPU-2-core budget. MMLU (14k), truthfulqa (817×104 targets), gsm8k (1319 with long generations) are each comparable to or larger than WikiText-2.\n- Therefore: staged/priority design. Tier 0 (exactness gate, ~1 h): BF16 ppl on 1 WikiText slice + rotated-no-quant check. Tier 1 (core result, all 12 cells): WikiText-2 ppl + a *small fixed subset* of each zero-shot task (e.g., 25% random subsample, fixed across cells, seed-fixed) → gives ranking with paired-subset comparison. Tier 2 (only 4-bit cells + FP16 + Identity-4bit): full zero-shot battery. Tier 3 (optional): LLaMA-3-8B positive control, groupsize/damp sensitivity, random-rotation arm. Report a table of which tiers are affordable in the given budget.\n- Sub-sampling caveat: with 25% subsets, task SE roughly doubles (×2); state the minimum detectable effect and pre-commit to the paired-difference (McNemar) test on the same examples rather than independent proportions — McNemar on paired binary outcomes is much more powerful and is the right control here.\n- Disk: use /tmp tmpfs (1 TB) for weights/Hessians; keep /workspace for metrics only (6 GB free).\n- Software risk: Python 3.14 vs QuaRot's pinned torch 2.2/2.3 + transformers 4.40–4.43 (phi3 support landed in 4.41+); building from source on 3.14 is a real risk → require a py3.10/3.11 conda env on the execution host.\n\nE. Assumptions & open choices, and where the brief is silent\n1. Weights: assume access to `microsoft/Phi-3-mini-4k-instruct` BF16 from a mirror/cache on the execution host; nothing exists in this environment (verified).\n2. Model config numbers above (head_dim 96, kv_heads 32, vocab 32064, longrope, tie_word_embeddings, attention scaling) are from memory of the release config and must be verified against `config.json` before implementing; in particular the brief's \"GQA ratio\" premise appears not to hold for Phi-3-mini.\n3. QuaRot source: assume upstream astone3207/QuaRot at the NeurIPS-2024 release; my code-specificity claims are from model knowledge of that repo, NOT from inspection (repo absent here). If the intended codebase is a different fork (e.g., GPT-FAQ, KIVI/OmniQuant, or an internal port), the adaptation list must be re-derived.\n4. Scope: weight-only quantization (W4/W6/W8), KV cache and activations in FP16. The research question contrasts RTN vs GPTQ (both weight-rounding algorithms), so weight-only is the coherent reading; the brief is silent on W4A4/W4A16 — flag as an open choice (W4A4 would