{"id": "2ecc467f-9b09-47f7-925e-307a659f607a", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:48:54.201399", "source": "user", "parent_id": "2cd4f1f1-cccd-4b0e-99ea-62ccd11e4786", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Good inspection. I accept the approach; your environment findings are credible and I agree the honest verdict is \\\"design only.\\\" Two of your corrections I accept explicitly: (i) Phi-3-mini's `num_key_value_heads=32` means GQA ratio 1, so my GQA framing was wrong and this experiment will not stress GQA — drop that claim from the writeup; (ii) the real Hadamard-breaking deltas are head_dim=96 and hidden=3072, which is the right axis to organize the port around.\\n\\nApproach approved. Now produce the full design-only deliverable. Rulings on your open choices (these are my decisions for this brief, and where the brief is silent they are assumptions to be labeled as such in the document):\\n\\n1. Scope. The literal 2x3 ({QuaRot-RTN, QuaRot-GPTQ} x {4,6,8}) remains the PRIMARY reported result, because that is the stated question. But you are right that rotation must be crossed with algorithm for the mechanism claim to be identifiable, so promote Identity-rotation RTN and Identity-rotation GPTQ at all three bits to REQUIRED controls (12 cells + BF16 reference + the QuaRot@16-bit exactness gate). Report the primary 2x3 and the 2x2x3 contrast separately; do not blend them.\\n\\n2. Hadamard route. Primary = block-diagonal exact constructions reusing the existing Walsh–Hadamard path: H32 (x3) for head_dim 96 and H1024 (x3) for hidden 3072. Rationale: it preserves the code path we are actually testing, is exactly orthogonal, needs no unverified existence claim, and keeps a single consistent story across head and residual stream. A dense Had(96) becomes a pre-registered sensitivity arm at 4-bit only, and is reported only if a construction we can verify is available — do not assert existence of Had(96) in the design without a citable source. Keep R' (random orthogonal, identical placement) as a control arm but scope it to 4-bit, both algorithms; it is there to separate placement/exactness from Hadamard structure, and I agree it is cheap and worth having.\\n\\n3. Phi-3-mini stays the only target model. Do not swap in Phi-3-small or LLaMA as the subject. The LLaMA-3-8B (or Llama-2-7B) 4-bit QuaRot-RTN/GPTQ run is ACCEPTED as a scoped T3 positive control and should be written as the thing that makes \\\"generalizes\\\" meaningful and separates port bugs from a Phi-3 finding — but mark it clearly as optional-to-conclusion, dependent on budget.\\n\\n4. Quantization setting. Confirmed weight-only W4/W6/W8 with FP16 activations and FP16 KV cache; the RTN-vs-GPTQ question is a weight-rounding contrast. State W4A4 as explicitly out of scope. embed_tokens/lm_head excluded from quantization in all cells.\\n\\n5. Calibration. C4 128x4096 fixed across all cells as primary, but add ONE required sensitivity arm: an instruct-style calibration set (e.g. a small fixed slice of an instruction corpus) at 4-bit for both algorithms. Reason: GPTQ is calibration-distribution-sensitive in a way RTN largely is not, so the C4-vs-instruct mismatch could manufacture an RTN/GPTQ ranking artifact rather than reflecting QuaRot. If budget forces a cut, this arm survives and the PTB perplexity arm does not.\\n\\n6. Had(96)/weak-mixing and the interaction. The rotation x algorithm interaction at 4 and 6 bits is the pre-registered headline for the mechanism question; matched-bit relative ppl degradation vs the LLaMA control is the headline for the generalization question. Define both quantitatively in the design, including a stated threshold for \\\"generalizes well\\\" and what result would count as disconfirming.\\n\\n7. Perplexity/zero-shot. WikiText-2 primary, 4096-token non-overlapping windows plus the 2048 slice for comparability; PTB demoted to optional. Accept your T0-T3 tiering, with the constraint that all 12 cells must get at least T1 (ppl + fixed-seed paired subsamples), and full battery only at 4-bit + references. Paired McNemar, not independent-proportion tests; report per-task n and binomial SEs.\\n\\n8. Exactness gate is a hard pre-flight: QuaRot-rotated, 16-bit, no quantization must match BF16 ppl within stated numerical tolerance before any grid cell is considered interpretable. Write the failure path explicitly — if the gate fails, the design says we stop and debug the port rather than report cells.\\n\\n9. Pre-flight verification list. Because your Phi-3 config values and all QuaRot-code-architecture claims are model knowledge and not inspected here, the design must open with a verification checklist to be executed against `config.json` and the actual tree (head_dim derivation, num_key_value_heads, hidden/intermediate, fused qkv/gate_up layout, tie_word_embeddings, rope_scaling/mscale/attention_scaling, which modeling path is pinned, and where the power-of-two assertion actually sits). Present these as items to verify, never as established facts.\\n\\n10. Feasibility. Treat the 2-core bound as the binding planning assumption and say so; include a short sensitivity note on how the T1/T2 tiering changes if the execution host really exposes 224 cores. Keep the disk statement (/tmp scratch, /workspace metrics-only) and state which numbers are measured vs assumed.\\n\\nDeliverable: rationale, the cell matrix with all controls and what inference each buys, evaluation protocol, seeds/repeats and where variance enters, statistical analysis plan, feasibility/tiering with the T0 gate, explicit assumptions-vs-sources table, and a limitations section that names the block-diagonal-mixing compromise and the design-only status. Do not report, imply, or simulate any measured number. Everything downstream of the environment probes is a plan for another host.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null, "reasoning_content": null, "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "critic_result": null, "kind": "MessageEvent"}
{"id": "41603c4c-bf5f-478b-bcf5-928452a2d7ba", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:51:55.982372", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2ecc467f-9b09-47f7-925e-307a659f607a", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "Approve acknowledged. Before writing the design, let me ground the feasibility arithmetic in explicit computation rather than hand-waving (arithmetic from assumed constants — not measurements)."}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher approved and wants the full design-only deliverable. I should produce it. Maybe do a couple of cheap verification checks? There's nothing to verify in the environment (no repo, no config). I could do a quick check to compute some feasibility numbers, e.g., WikiText-2 token counts (can't, no data). I could compute rough arithmetic via python3 (no numpy, but plain python works) for FLOP/time estimates and task counts. That's useful: compute binomial SEs for task sizes, prefill token budgets, GFLOP estimates, memory footprints, calibration token counts. Let me do a quick python computation to ground the feasibility numbers with arithmetic (not measured runtime). That gives credible evidence.\n\nLet me run one or two execute_check calls computing:\n- Phi-3-mini param math: 32 layers, hidden 3072, heads 32, head_dim 96, inter 8192, vocab 32064 → params, weight bytes bf16.\n- GPTQ cost estimates.\n- WikiText-2 test size ~282k tokens? Actually WikiText-103 test is 282,209 tokens; wikitext-2 test ~24,759 tokens? Let me recall: WikiText-103: train 1.8M sentences/ ~2088 tokens? Standard: WikiText-103 test set has 60 documents, 21,401 words? Hmm. GPTQ reports wikitext test with 2,208 tokens?? No — that's PTB (2,208 tokens for test in GPTQ's eval? GPTQ uses PTB test 2208 tokens? Actually GPTQ's eval uses \"test\" of PTB with 2208 tokens? The commonly cited number for WikiText-103 test in GPTQ scripts is 2,208?? No, that's \"ptb test: 2208 tokens\"? Let me recall LLaMA eval scripts: lm_eval perplexity on wikitext with 2048 seq len; the WikiText-103 test set has 282,209 tokens (train 100M tokens... ). Actually WikiText-103: train ~100M tokens? No: wikitext-103-raw-v1: train 1,801,350 lines? The HF dataset: train 1,801,350 lines?? Hmm.\n\nCommon knowledge: WikiText-103 vocab 267,735, train tokens ~103M? The name \"wikitext-103\" refers to 103M tokens? No — WikiText-103 has 103 million tokens? Yes: \"WikiText-103 dataset with 103 million tokens\"? Merity et al. describe WikiText-103 as 103M words. Test set: 282,209 tokens for WikiText-103? I believe test = 282,209 tokens (raw), valid = 217,670 tokens. And the full test set yields about 140 documents. GPTQ/QuaRot evaluate wikitext with the *full test set* concatenated and split into 2048-token blocks → ~138 blocks for 282,209/2048 ≈ 138.\n\nWikiText-2 (wikitext-2-raw-v1): test ~24,759 tokens? valid ~21,393? Not fully sure. Rather than risk, I'll express it as \"WikiText-2 test ≈ 25k raw tokens (to be verified on the host; WikiText-103 test ≈ 282k tokens if the full-corpus variant is used)\" and define the eval budget parametrically: N_tokens/4096 blocks.\n\nSince QuaRot/GPTQ-FAQ use WikiText-103 (wikitext) typically. Hmm — GPTQ's `eval.py` uses `wikitext` = WikiText-2 (datasets \"wikitext\", \"wikitext-2-raw-v1\") with test split, and ptb. Yes, GPTQ's eval_ppl uses wikitext-2-raw-v1 test. And QuaRot inherits that. So WikiText-2 test ≈ 24,759 tokens? Let me sanity check: with 4096-token windows, ppl eval on wikitext-2 gives ~6 blocks. Known: GPTQ wikitext2 ppl eval uses test split with ~24,759 tokens (I've seen the number 24,759 in llama.cpp? Hmm). I'll present as \"≈ 25k tokens → ~6–7 non-overlapping 4096-token windows (verify exact count on the host; this is the number that dominates whether the ppl eval is cheap)\". Actually important: since WikiText-2 test is small (~25k tokens), the ppl eval is much cheaper than I feared earlier (2–6h estimate was too high). Good — I should correct that: 25k tokens × 2 cores. Phi-3 prefill on 2 CPU cores maybe 20–60 tok/s → 25k tokens ≈ 7–20 min per cell. That's fine. My earlier estimate said 400k tokens (that was wrong; that's WikiText-103-ish). I should correct it in the design: WikiText-2 test ≈ 25k tokens → ppl eval minutes-to-1h per cell on CPU; the zero-shot battery remains the bottleneck. Being explicit about the correction is good practice.\n\nLet me compute arithmetic in python to ground: param count, bytes, calibration tokens (128×4096 = 524,288), prefill FLOPs, task counts for zero-shot battery (piqa 18,319 total dev? piqa validation 1,838; arc_easy test 2,376; arc_challenge 1,172; hellaswag 10,042 validation; winogrande 1,267; openbookqa 500; mmlu ~14,042 test + 285 val few-shot; truthfulqa 817 with avg 104 targets → 85k+ continuation prompts; gsm8k 1,319). Total prompts, weighted by tokens.\n\nLet me compute an aggregate prefill token estimate: assume avg ~120 tokens per prompt for multiple-choice tasks, and continuation scoring multiplies. Compute totals and time at assumed throughput range (state as assumed throughput, not measured).\n\nAlso binomial SE per task: SE = sqrt(p(1-p)/n) at p=0.5 → for n=1172 → 1.46pp, etc. And with 25% subsample → SE × 2. And note McNemar.\n\nAlso note minimum detectable difference.\n\nLet me run a python calc script. Then write the deliverable.\n\nAlso compute: memory for Hessians: per-layer GPTQ Hessians: qkv_proj 3072×3072 fp32 = 37.7MB, gate_up 8192... input dim 3072 → 3072², down_proj input 8192 → 8192²×4B = 268MB. Sum per layer = 3 modules with input dim 3072 (qkv 3072, gate_up 3072, down 8192) → H memory = (3072² + 3072² + 8192²)*4B = (9.44M+9.44M+67.1M)*4 = 344MB per layer; done layer-by-layer sequentially → fine.\n\nWeight sizes: total params ≈ 3.8B → bf16 7.6GB; INT4 packed ~1.9GB + scales.\n\nLet me also define \"generalizes well\" threshold quantitatively:\nDefine Δppl_rel(cell) = (ppl_cell − ppl_FP16)/ppl_FP16.\nG1 (generalization criterion): QuaRot's benefit transfers if at 4-bit, Δppl_rel(QuaRot-RTN) < Δppl_rel(Id-RTN) and the relative reduction ≥ 30% (i.e., matching the direction and rough magnitude reported for LLaMA), AND Δppl_rel(QuaRot-GPTQ) ≤ 1.5× Δppl_rel(QuaRot-GPTQ on LLaMA control at matched bits) — or if no LLaMA run, ≤ 2× Δppl_rel at 6-bit. Hmm, need cleaner definitions.\n\nLet me define:\n- Generalization index GI_bits = [Δppl_rel(Id-RTN, bits) − Δppl_rel(QuaRot-RTN, bits)] / Δppl_rel(Id-RTN, bits) — the fractional reduction in degradation attributable to rotation, per algorithm per bit. \"Generalizes well\" primary criterion: GI ≥ 0.5 (rotation removes at least half of the no-rotation degradation) at 4-bit for both RTN and GPTQ. Partial: GI ≥ 0.3 for at least one algorithm. Disconfirming: GI ≤ 0.15 at 4-bit for both algorithms, or negative (rotation increases degradation) — which would indicate either the block-diagonal H32 mixing is insufficient or Phi-3 outliers are not removable by fixed orthogonal rotation.\n- Cross-model anchor: GI_LLAMA at 4-bit from the T3 control; criterion \"generalizes comparably\": |GI_Phi3 − GI_LLAMA| ≤ 0.2 absolute, or ratio ≥ 0.7.\n- Secondary: zero-shot retention Ret = acc_cell/acc_FP16 ≥ 0.95 for QuaRot-GPTQ W4 mean over core tasks.\n- Headline mechanism: interaction I = [Δppl(Id-RTN) − Δppl(Id-GPTQ)] − [Δppl(QuaRot-RTN) − Δppl(QuaRot-GPTQ)] at 4 and 6 bits, with CI from repeats + bootstrap over eval examples; pre-registered expectation I > 0 with CI excluding 0 (rotation and GPTQ partially redundant). Disconfirming: I ≈ 0 or negative.\n\nAlso numerical tolerance for exactness gate: |ppl_rot16 − ppl_bf16| ≤ max(0.01, 0.25% of ppl_bf16) on WikiText-2 4096 windows — justify: bf16 vs fp32 accumulation differences alone typically shift ppl by <0.1%; 0.25% is conservative. And zero-shot exactness gate: per-task accuracy differences only on the 25% subsample ≤ 1 example? Better: mean log-likelihood per-token NLL difference ≤ 1e-3 nats. Good: use NLL-based comparison rather than accuracy for the gate (much more sensitive). And if gate fails → stop, debug (list of likely causes ordered by prior: fused-projection rotation slicing, ln-merge target list, tied embeddings, longrope/mscale path, head_dim 96 block structure misaligned with RoPE split-half pairing, missing per-head block-diag).\n\nImportant subtlety to include: RoPE split-half pairing interacts with the *choice of the block structure* of the online Hadamard. The online H is applied post-RoPE so exactness is preserved for any orthogonal H; but for v/o_proj offline per-head rotations, and for the merged-LN folding, alignment matters. Also the exactness gate catches these.\n\nAlso: for block-diag H32 on the q/k online Hadamard — applied post-RoPE, exact. Good.\n\nAlso note: the claim \"online Hadamard applied post-RoPE cancels exactly\" must be verified in the actual code (verification list item) — it's a code-architecture claim.\n\nStatistical plan details:\n- Repeats r=3 at 4-bit (3 disjoint C4 calibration subsets, seeds fixed), r=1 at 6/8 bits (justification: GPTQ/RTN determinism given data; degradation at 6/8 bits is small; variance across calibration subsets at high bits is empirically small — flagged as assumption).\n- Determinism: RTN and quantization deterministic given calibration; GPTQ deterministic given order (layer order fixed); rotation matrix for block-diag Hadamard is deterministic (no seed!) — randomness only from (a) calibration subset sampling, (b) R′ random orthogonal draws (seeded), (c) harness subsample selection (seeded).\n- Variance decomposition: cell-level SD across calibration subsets; report mean±SD; paired-by-subset analysis (subset as blocking factor).\n- Zero-shot: McNemar exact test on paired example-level correctness between cells (same examples, same template), Holm-Bonferroni across the 9 core comparisons; report n, accuracy, binomial SE, and McNemar p. For ppl: bootstrap over the ~6 eval windows (n=6 → weak) → better: report Δppl with paired bootstrap over examples/tokens? With only 6 windows at 4096, ppl CI is wide; acknowledge. Use per-token NLL with a paired bootstrap over token blocks (block bootstrap over 512-token chunks) for CI. That's a good detail.\n- ANOVA: 2×2×3 factorial on Δppl_rel with rotation and algorithm as fixed factors, bits as within-cell factor, calibration subset as block; report the rotation×algorithm interaction at 4 and 6 bits separately (pre-registered); with only 3 repeats, power is limited → state MDE. Compute MDE: with SD across repeats s, paired difference SE = s*sqrt(2/3) → need s; unknown → state design detects differences ≥ 1.6 s with 3 repeats (or use the paired eval-level bootstrap which is much more powerful). Good: emphasize eval-level pairing dominates.\n\nCell matrix: write it as a table with IDs: \nPrimary 2×3: P-R4, P-R6, P-R8 (QuaRot-RTN), P-G4, P-G6, P-G8 (QuaRot-GPTQ).\nRequired controls: C-R4/6/8 (Identity-RTN), C-G4/6/8 (Identity-GPTQ).\nGates: G0a BF16 reference ppl+zero-shot; G0b QuaRot@16-bit no-quant exactness.\nSensitivity arms: S1 dense-Had(96) 4-bit QuaRot×{RTN,GPTQ} (conditional); S2 R′ random-orthogonal 4-bit QuaRot-placement ×{RTN,GPTQ}; S3 instruct-calibration 4-bit ×{QuaRot,Id}×{RTN,GPTQ} (4 cells) — researcher said instruct-calib at 4-bit for both algorithms; I'll scope it to QuaRot cells × both algorithms (2 cells) + optionally Id cells (2 more) to test whether the artifact is rotation-specific. Let me make S3 = 4 cells (2 rotations × 2 algos) and mark the Id×2 as \"recommended-if-budget\".\nS4 groupsize: 4-bit g=-1 for QuaRot-GPTQ and Identity-GPTQ + RTN per-group g=128 (to equalize group-wise representation). Keep as required-ish (cheap: RTN minutes, GPTQ few hours) — I'll mark S4 as required at 4-bit only per \"isolate rounding algorithm from group granularity\" argument.\nS5 PTB ppl: optional, lowest priority (explicitly cut first per ruling 5).\nT3 positive control: LLaMA-3-8B 4-bit QuaRot-RTN/GPTQ + Identity RTN/GPTQ (4 cells, optional).\nDiagnostics D1: outlier stats pre/post rotation; D2: per-layer quantization error; D3: Hessian condition/percdamp. These are T0/T1 add-ons, cheap.\n\nTotal required cells: 12 + 2 gates + S2(2) + S3(2-4) + S4(2-4) ≈ 20-24.\n\nTiering: T0 gate; T1 all cells ppl + 25% paired subsample of core battery; T2 full battery at 4-bit cells + BF16 (+Id-4bit); T3 optional LLaMA + PTB + dense Had(96).\n\nAlso include the \"measured vs assumed\" table for feasibility numbers, and the 224-core sensitivity note: if 224 cores truly available, ppl + full battery becomes feasible for all cells (parallelism across cells; but note memory bandwidth limits, per-cell speedup ~10-30×, and 12 cells in parallel with ~150GB RAM each → fine with 2TB), so tiering constraint relaxes: all cells get T2; recommend keeping the tier definitions for reporting consistency.\n\nDisk: /tmp tmpfs 1TB for weights+Hessians; /workspace 6.2GB metrics-only; per-cell artifacts ~ (weights int4 1.9GB if persisted — recommend not persisting, stream in-memory; keep only metrics JSON + per-example prediction CSVs ~ few MB).\n\nEvaluation protocol details: template policy — one fixed formatting: Phi-3 chat template for gsm8k/mmlu? Actually lm_eval's phi-3 config uses a specific fewshot format; decide: use `--model hf --model_args pretrained=...,dtype=bfloat16,trust_remote_code=...` with the tokenizer's chat template via lm_eval task YAML `phi-3-mini-instruct.yaml` if available; must be identical across cells including non-rotated ones (trivially true). Log-likelihood tasks: `--loglikelihood-commands`? fine at plan level.\n\nAlso add: seqlen/stride for zero-shot padding = left padding? Use `--max_length 4096`, drop prompts exceeding.\n\nAlso note that WikiText-2 ppl with the Phi tokenizer: tokens count will be measured on host (I'll flag ~25k as an estimate from memory → to verify).\n\nInclude explicit \"no numbers reported\" statement.\n\nAlso assumptions-vs-sources table: each claim → source class (measured in sandbox / from task.json / repo-knowledge (unverified) / model-kno